          "text": "EVORAG | DBMS & SQL KNOWLEDGE BASE POSTGRESQL 18 REFERENCE BASELINE Educational retrieval corpus-original explanations with cited references Page 4 4. Distributed consistency and the CAP principle Distributed systems communicate over networks that can delay, drop, or partition messages. During a network partition, nodes on opposite sides may be unable to coordinate. The CAP principle is commonly summarized in terms of Consistency, Availability, and Partition tolerance. In its precise use, it concerns trade-offs during a partition: a system cannot guarantee both a single-copy consistency model and availability for every request across the partition. Partition tolerance is generally necessary in a real distributed deployment because network partitions cannot simply be ruled out. CAP is not a universal rule that a database can permanently choose any two of three features.",
